WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.537
Transport Layer Security is one of the basic trust mechanisms behind modern web communication.

00:00:05.537 --> 00:00:10.197
When a browser connects to a site, an application calls an API,

00:00:10.197 --> 00:00:12.915
a mobile app talks to a backend,

00:00:12.915 --> 00:00:16.410
or one service calls another inside a cloud environment,

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:20.681
TLS is often the protocol that provides confidentiality, integrity, and authentication.

00:00:20.681 --> 00:00:25.341
It also appears at load balancers, API gateways, reverse proxies, SaaS platforms,

00:00:25.341 --> 00:00:26.895
ingress controllers, service meshes,

00:00:26.895 --> 00:00:30.390
and internal applications that never face the public internet.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:36.603
Post-quantum cryptography matters here because TLS relies on public-key cryptography during important parts of the handshake.

00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:40.486
Public-key mechanisms help authenticate the server and establish key material.

00:00:40.486 --> 00:00:43.204
Once the handshake has done its job,

00:00:43.204 --> 00:00:48.641
TLS commonly uses symmetric encryption to protect the application data moving across the connection.

00:00:48.641 --> 00:00:54.078
That split is important: the quantum concern is most urgent for some public-key algorithms,

00:00:54.078 --> 00:00:57.572
while symmetric encryption and hashing have different planning considerations.

00:00:57.572 --> 00:01:01.067
The risk is not limited to a future outage.

00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:04.951
Harvest-now, decrypt-later scenarios matter when traffic is sensitive for years.

00:01:04.951 --> 00:01:09.611
An adversary may collect encrypted network traffic today and attempt to decrypt

00:01:09.611 --> 00:01:14.271
it later if the public-key protection used during the session becomes breakable.

00:01:14.271 --> 00:01:17.765
This is especially relevant for regulated information, government data,

00:01:17.765 --> 00:01:19.707
sensitive business records, intellectual property,

00:01:19.707 --> 00:01:23.590
and enterprise API traffic where confidentiality has a long lifetime.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:28.250
For TLS and web services, PQC is a migration and operations problem,

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.192
not just a cryptography problem.

00:01:30.192 --> 00:01:34.464
The work touches endpoint inventory, certificates, libraries, clients, mobile apps, proxies,

00:01:34.464 --> 00:01:37.959
monitoring, performance, change management, vendor readiness, and rollback planning.

00:01:37.959 --> 00:01:43.395
A useful program treats PQC as practical crypto agility: understand where TLS is used,

00:01:43.395 --> 00:01:46.113
prioritize based on risk, test standards-based mechanisms,

00:01:46.113 --> 00:01:49.220
and make change possible without breaking critical services.
